sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-18> delphi:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch05-18--gpt> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch05-18--gpt> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "GPT-5.6 Sol on thompson1917-ch05-18: needs-new-data, resolvability 0.44" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.443"^^xsd:double ;
    delphi:hasVerdict "needs-new-data" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.72"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "The geometric and adsorption mechanism has measurable consequences, but endpoint images cannot establish inevitability or causal assembly. Existing data are especially weak for separating physical formation from a protective evolutionary function." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: Image lorica formation in living protozoa while measuring material adsorption and boundary geometry, then test whether the developing pellicle minimizes area under measured constraints and whether blocking adsorption prevents formation. Protection would be assessed separately through survival experiments and cannot be excluded by geometric fit alone." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "Formation movies and adsorption measurements for the named taxa are scarce.", "Minimum-area fit does not distinguish efficient cause from adaptive function.", "Lorica materials and boundary conditions vary among taxa." ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/thompson1917-ch05-18--gpt--dataset-1> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch05-18--gpt--operationalizability> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
